---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout
  currentSlug="parity"
  title="Parity harness"
  description="How Corvene is compared against GitHub Desktop, screenshot by screenshot."
>
  <p>
    Corvene should look and behave like GitHub Desktop 3.6.6. To check that, <code>tools/parity</code> runs both apps
    side by side, sends them the same clicks and keys, screenshots both, and compares the screenshots pixel by pixel.
  </p>

  <h2 id="run">Running it</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={"pip3 install -r tools/parity/requirements.txt\ncargo build -p corvene --features snapshots\npython3 tools/parity/parity.py [scenario…]"} />
  <p>
    The report ends up in <code>target/parity/latest/index.html</code>, with each pair of screenshots overlaid, side by
    side, and as a swipe. The run fails if any screenshot differs by more than its threshold.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">What it does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Both apps run as private copies with fresh settings, so your own GitHub Desktop and Corvene are left alone. Neither
      needs focus; you can keep working while it runs.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each gets its own copy of a test repository with fixed authors and dates, so commit ids and "3 days ago" match.
    </li>
    <li>Screenshots are taken at 1367×814, in the dark and the light theme.</li>
    <li>
      Anti-aliasing differs between Chromium and Corvene, so pixels next to text get more tolerance than flat areas.
      A 1px shift or a slightly wrong colour still fails.
    </li>
    <li>
      Corvene runs with the <em>GitHub Desktop</em> <a href={`${base}/docs/flags`}>preset</a>, which switches off
      everything Corvene does differently.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A change to a view shouldn't make its scenarios worse, and anything new on screen gets a scenario of its own. The full
    details are in <code>tools/parity/README.md</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="linux">Linux and Windows</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub Desktop has no official Linux build, so on Linux Corvene uses the menus and wording of the community one. The
    window frame on Linux and Windows is newer than the macOS one and doesn't match pixel for pixel everywhere yet.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
